  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "A rock is thrown so that its height $h$ metres above the ground at time $t\\geq0$ second is given by the formula\n$$h=-5t^2+15t+20.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p22-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "What is the initial height of the rock?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$20\\text{ m}$",
          "worked": "$$h(0)=-5(0)^2+15(0)+20=20.$$\n\nThe initial height is $20\\text{ m}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "For what duration is the rock in the air?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$4\\text{ s}$",
          "worked": "At ground level, $h=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}-5t^2+15t+20&=0\\\\t^2-3t-4&=0\\\\(t-4)(t+1)&=0\\\\t&=4,-1.\\end{align*}$$Since $t\\geq0$, the rock is in the air for $4\\text{ s}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find the maximum height of the rock.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{125}{4}\\text{ m}$",
          "worked": "The parabola opens downwards. Its turning point lies halfway between the roots $-1$ and $4$.\n$$\\begin{align*}t&=\\frac{-1+4}{2}=\\frac32\\\\h\\left(\\frac32\\right)&=-5\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2+15\\left(\\frac32\\right)+20\\\\h\\left(\\frac32\\right)&=\\frac{125}{4}.\\end{align*}$$The maximum height is $\\dfrac{125}{4}\\text{ m}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q5-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graph of the height $h$ of the rock.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Downward-opening parabola through $(0,20)$ and $(4,0)$, with turning point $\\left(\\dfrac32,\\dfrac{125}{4}\\right)$, for $0\\leq t\\leq4$.",
          "worked": "Use the initial point $(0,20)$, ground-level point $(4,0)$ and maximum point $\\left(\\dfrac32,\\dfrac{125}{4}\\right)$. Sketch a downward-opening parabola over the flight interval $0\\leq t\\leq4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p22-q5-d-solution-graph",
              "role": "solution",
              "format": "tikz",
              "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"page 22 question 5(d); teacher page 9\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.05cm,y=0.12cm]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (0,0)--(4.8,0) node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,right] {$t\\ (\\mathrm{s})$};\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (0,0)--(0,37) node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,above] {$h\\ (\\mathrm{m})$};\n\\draw[line width=0.8pt,domain=0:4,samples=81] plot (\\x,{-5*\\x*\\x+15*\\x+20});\n\\draw[line width=0.4pt] (4,0)--(4,-0.8);\n\\node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,below] at (4,0) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$4$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};\n\\draw[line width=0.4pt] (0,20)--(-0.07,20);\n\\node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,left] at (0,20) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$20$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};\n\\fill (1.5,31.25) circle (1pt);\n\\node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,above] at (1.5,31.25) {$\\left(\\frac32,\\frac{125}{4}\\right)$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
              "src": null,
              "widthMm": 65,
              "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
              "overlayOf": null,
              "transparent": false,
              "axes": null,
              "spec": {
                "sourcePage": 22,
                "sourceRegion": "Question 5(d); answer graph on teacher PDF page 9, printed page 338",
                "description": "Unshaded equation-driven flight graph with height and time axes, initial height, landing time and labelled maximum.",
                "mathematics": "h=-5t^2+15t+20 for 0<=t<=4; vertex (3/2,125/4).",
                "answerVisibility": "Solution only; no graph is supplied in the student question."
              },
              "derived": false,
              "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
            }
          ]
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q5-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Find the times when the rock is exactly $25\\text{ m}$ above ground level.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$t=\\dfrac{3-\\sqrt5}{2}\\text{ s}$ and $t=\\dfrac{3+\\sqrt5}{2}\\text{ s}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}-5t^2+15t+20&=25\\\\-5t^2+15t-5&=0\\\\t^2-3t+1&=0\\\\t&=\\frac{3\\pm\\sqrt{(-3)^2-4(1)(1)}}{2}\\\\t&=\\frac{3\\pm\\sqrt5}{2}.\\end{align*}$$Both times are within the flight interval. The rock is at $25\\text{ m}$ after $\\dfrac{3-\\sqrt5}{2}\\text{ s}$ and $\\dfrac{3+\\sqrt5}{2}\\text{ s}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q5-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "The rock is at least $H$ metres above the ground for exactly $1$ second. Find $H$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p22-q5-f-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "H=30\\text{ m}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p22-q5-f-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The one-second interval is centred on the turning-point time, so its endpoints are 1 s and 2 s.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The graph is symmetric about $t=\\dfrac32$. The one-second interval above the level $H$ therefore has endpoints\n$$\\begin{align*}t_1&=\\frac32-\\frac12=1\\\\t_2&=\\frac32+\\frac12=2\\\\H&=h(1)\\\\H&=-5(1)^2+15(1)+20\\\\H&=30.\\end{align*}$$Also $h(2)=30$, and the rock is at least $30\\text{ m}$ above ground for $1\\leq t\\leq2$, a duration of exactly $1\\text{ s}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      }
    ]
  },
